<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<math xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML" display="block">
  <!--
    DaemonPatterns.mml
    Declarative dialogue and pattern-processing contract for cooperating daemons.

    Messages carry typed acts and references. Visible utterances are presentation
    text; they are not executable commands or transferable authority.
  -->

  <semantics>
    <mrow>
      <mi>DialogueEvent</mi><mo>=</mo>
      <mo>&#x27E8;</mo><mi>conversation</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>turn</mi><mo>,</mo>
      <mi>sender</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>recipient</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>act</mi><mo>,</mo>
      <mi>phase</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>evidenceRefs</mi><mo>,</mo>
      <mi>utterance</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>provenance</mi><mo>&#x27E9;</mo>
    </mrow>
    <annotation-xml encoding="MathML-Content">
      <apply>
        <csymbol cd="urn:kuhul:daemon-patterns:v1">DialogueEvent</csymbol>
        <list>
          <ci>conversation</ci><ci>turn</ci><ci>sender</ci><ci>recipient</ci>
          <ci>act</ci><ci>phase</ci><ci>evidenceRefs</ci><ci>utterance</ci><ci>provenance</ci>
        </list>
      </apply>
    </annotation-xml>
    <annotation encoding="text/plain">
speech acts include request, question, hypothesis, evidence, objection,
challenge, answer, proposal, agreement, disagreement, and final_response.
utterance is display text. Typed fields and referenced evidence carry protocol meaning.
    </annotation>
  </semantics>

  <mspace linebreak="newline"/>

  <semantics>
    <mrow>
      <mi>PromptField</mi><mo>=</mo>
      <mo>&#x27E8;</mo><mi>userPrompt</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>keyDefinitions</mi><mo>,</mo>
      <mi>aimlMatches</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>gramEvidence</mi><mo>,</mo>
      <mi>vectorEvidence</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>externalEvidence</mi><mo>&#x27E9;</mo>
    </mrow>
    <annotation-xml encoding="MathML-Content">
      <apply>
        <csymbol cd="urn:kuhul:daemon-patterns:v1">PromptField</csymbol>
        <list><ci>userPrompt</ci><ci>keyDefinitions</ci><ci>aimlMatches</ci>
          <ci>gramEvidence</ci><ci>vectorEvidence</ci><ci>externalEvidence</ci></list>
      </apply>
    </annotation-xml>
    <annotation encoding="text/plain">
Each source occupies a provenance-tagged field lane. Key definitions and AIML
matches contribute evidence; they do not silently become verified facts.
Unigrams, bigrams, and trigrams may support priors; longer grams may retrieve
frameworks or history. No gram is executable merely because it matched.
    </annotation>
  </semantics>

  <mspace linebreak="newline"/>

  <semantics>
    <mrow>
      <mi>DialogueCycle</mi><mo>=</mo>
      <mi>observe</mi><mo>&#x2192;</mo><mi>field</mi><mo>&#x2192;</mo>
      <mi>question</mi><mo>&#x2192;</mo><mi>candidate</mi><mo>&#x2192;</mo>
      <mi>critic</mi><mo>&#x2192;</mo><mi>verify</mi><mo>&#x2192;</mo><mi>respond</mi>
    </mrow>
    <annotation-xml encoding="MathML-Content">
      <apply>
        <csymbol cd="urn:kuhul:daemon-patterns:v1">BoundedDialogueCycle</csymbol>
        <list><ci>observe</ci><ci>field</ci><ci>question</ci><ci>candidate</ci>
          <ci>critic</ci><ci>verify</ci><ci>respond</ci></list>
      </apply>
    </annotation-xml>
    <annotation encoding="text/plain">
Neural Grammar Core may classify intent, match grammar, and ask clarifying
questions. KHANARY may retrieve field evidence and propose candidate responses.
Exploration patterns may challenge candidates; critic patterns assess their
support. The response owner emits the final answer or a necessary question.
    </annotation>
  </semantics>

  <mspace linebreak="newline"/>

  <semantics>
    <mrow>
      <mi>QuestionIdentity</mi><mo>=</mo>
      <mo>&#x27E8;</mo><mi>questionId</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>origin</mi><mo>,</mo>
      <mi>target</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>intentGap</mi><mo>,</mo>
      <mi>parentQuestionId</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>status</mi><mo>&#x27E9;</mo>
    </mrow>
    <annotation-xml encoding="MathML-Content">
      <apply>
        <csymbol cd="urn:kuhul:daemon-patterns:v1">QuestionRecord</csymbol>
        <list><ci>questionId</ci><ci>origin</ci><ci>target</ci><ci>intentGap</ci>
          <ci>parentQuestionId</ci><ci>status</ci></list>
      </apply>
    </annotation-xml>
    <annotation encoding="text/plain">
Question IDs are stable within the conversation. Status progresses among
proposed, asked, answered, resolved, superseded, and unresolved. Parent links
identify follow-ups and support duplicate detection and replay.
    </annotation>
  </semantics>

  <mspace linebreak="newline"/>

  <semantics>
    <mrow>
      <mi>Stop</mi><mo>=</mo>
      <mi>supportedAnswer</mi><mo>&#x2228;</mo><mi>clarificationRequired</mi>
      <mo>&#x2228;</mo><mi>budgetExhausted</mi><mo>&#x2228;</mo><mi>cancelled</mi>
    </mrow>
    <annotation-xml encoding="MathML-Content">
      <apply>
        <csymbol cd="urn:kuhul:daemon-patterns:v1">TerminationCondition</csymbol>
        <or/><ci>supportedAnswer</ci><ci>clarificationRequired</ci>
        <ci>budgetExhausted</ci><ci>cancelled</ci>
      </apply>
    </annotation-xml>
    <annotation encoding="text/plain">
Every request and response consumes a bounded dialogue turn. Repeated or
equivalent questions are deduplicated. A daemon may return uncertainty instead
of manufacturing consensus when evidence is insufficient or budgets expire.
    </annotation>
  </semantics>

  <mspace linebreak="newline"/>

  <semantics>
    <mrow>
      <mi>PeerMessage</mi><mo>&#x2260;</mo><mi>Admission</mi>
      <mspace width="1em"/>
      <mi>Proposal</mi><mo>&#x2260;</mo><mi>Execution</mi>
    </mrow>
    <annotation-xml encoding="MathML-Content">
      <apply>
        <csymbol cd="urn:kuhul:daemon-patterns:v1">AuthoritySeparation</csymbol>
        <ci>PeerMessage</ci><ci>Admission</ci><ci>Proposal</ci><ci>Execution</ci>
      </apply>
    </annotation-xml>
    <annotation encoding="text/plain">
A peer cannot grant its recipient capabilities or transfer an admission decision.
Requested actions must be validated against local registration, delegated scope,
resource budget, and XCFE policy at the execution boundary. Memory and learning
updates retain provenance and require their own authorized commit path.
    </annotation>
  </semantics>

  <mspace linebreak="newline"/>

  <semantics>
    <mrow>
      <mi>DistilledPattern</mi><mo>=</mo>
      <mo>&#x27E8;</mo><mi>source</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>topic</mi><mo>,</mo>
      <mi>phase</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>aimlPair</mi><mo>,</mo>
      <mi>discussionTemplate</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>provenance</mi><mo>&#x27E9;</mo>
    </mrow>
    <annotation-xml encoding="MathML-Content">
      <apply>
        <csymbol cd="urn:kuhul:daemon-patterns:v1">DistilledPattern</csymbol>
        <list><ci>source</ci><ci>topic</ci><ci>phase</ci>
          <ci>aimlPair</ci><ci>discussionTemplate</ci><ci>provenance</ci></list>
      </apply>
    </annotation-xml>
    <annotation encoding="text/plain">
A distilled pattern is an LLM-generated AIML pair or discussion template produced
by tools/gemma-distill.py and stored in data/alice/khanary-distilled.aiml or
data/micronaut/distilled-questions.json. source=gemma-distill; provenance chain
is preserved. Distilled patterns are candidate evidence, not verified facts; they
enter the DialogueCycle as gramEvidence in the PromptField, not as admitted rules.
    </annotation>
  </semantics>

  <mspace linebreak="newline"/>

  <semantics>
    <mrow>
      <mi>DaemonRole</mi><mo>=</mo>
      <mo>&#x27E8;</mo><mi>name</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>kind</mi><mo>,</mo>
      <mi>phase</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>temperature</mi><mo>,</mo><mi>role</mi><mo>&#x27E9;</mo>
    </mrow>
    <annotation-xml encoding="MathML-Content">
      <apply>
        <csymbol cd="urn:kuhul:daemon-patterns:v1">DaemonRole</csymbol>
        <list><ci>name</ci><ci>kind</ci><ci>phase</ci><ci>temperature</ci><ci>role</ci></list>
      </apply>
    </annotation-xml>
    <annotation encoding="text/plain">
Each daemon carries a declared phase, sampling temperature, and role string.
Phase-driver daemons (pop/wo/yax/sek/chen/xul) bias the LLM toward the
phase-appropriate epistemic posture. Specialist daemons (eliza, alice, think,
reason, expand, coder, librarian) carry phase-specific capabilities declared in
drivers/micronauts/*.json. Temperature is an evidence-generation parameter, not
authority. DaemonRole records are read-only at dispatch time; the MML contract
governing each daemon's authority boundary lives in data/grammar/daemon-{name}.mml.
    </annotation>
  </semantics>

  <mspace linebreak="newline"/>

  <semantics>
    <mrow>
      <mi>Sheogorath</mi><mo>&#x29F8;</mo><mi>Jyggalag</mi>
      <mo>=</mo><mi>EntropyCyclePair</mi>
    </mrow>
    <annotation-xml encoding="MathML-Content">
      <apply>
        <csymbol cd="urn:kuhul:daemon-patterns:v1">EntropyCyclePair</csymbol>
        <ci>sheogorath</ci><ci>jyggalag</ci>
      </apply>
    </annotation-xml>
    <annotation encoding="text/plain">
Sheogorath (T=0.97, Xul phase) generates maximum-entropy candidate states.
Jyggalag (T=0.00, Ch'en phase) collapses the candidate set to a single surviving
state. Together they implement the entropy-collapse pattern: expand → verify →
collapse. Neither can commit topology independently; Xul commit authority requires
the full Proposal → Ch'en → XCFE → Xul path. The Greymarch event (total-order
collapse) is Jyggalag's terminal output record, not a topology mutation. This
pair must appear as a matched unit in any DialogueCycle that uses high-temperature
generation; Sheogorath without Jyggalag leaves the candidate set uncollapsed.
    </annotation>
  </semantics>

  <annotation encoding="text/plain">
DaemonPatterns contract invariants:
1. Human-visible dialogue and machine protocol fields are separate.
2. Every question, claim, and observation retains identity and provenance.
3. Pattern matches and retrieved vectors are evidence, not truth declarations.
4. Dialogue is bounded, deduplicated, replayable, and side-effect-free by default.
5. Daemon proposals never bypass local admission, XCFE, or host authorization.
6. This MML document describes a contract; it does not start a daemon or enable IPC.
7. Distilled patterns (gemma-distill provenance) enter as gramEvidence, not admitted rules.
8. DaemonRole temperature is an evidence parameter; it does not modify phase authority.
9. The EntropyCyclePair (Sheogorath/Jyggalag) must appear as a matched unit; uncollapsed entropy is not a terminal state.
  </annotation>
</math>
